---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ComparisonSection from "../components/ComparisonSection.astro";
import { builderVocabulary } from "../lib/builder";
import { canvasVocabulary } from "../lib/canvas";
import { starterViews } from "../lib/starters";
import manifest from "../../public/data/manifest.json";
import type { Manifest } from "../lib/data";

// The link to the starter canvas of true registrations is made from the starter's config, so it follows the charts it holds; the front page where
// this build holds none of them.
const registrations = starterViews(canvasVocabulary(builderVocabulary(manifest as Manifest)), manifest as Manifest, "/canvas/").find((s) => s.id === "registrations")?.href ?? "/";
---

<Layout title="Why these figures differ from others" activeNav="differences">
  <Fragment slot="masthead">
    <h1>Why these figures differ from others</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Two sources can both be right and still give different numbers, because they count different things. This
      page says what each one counts. It does not try to make the figures match.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>Registrations are not sales</h2>
    <p>
      A vehicle is counted in the month it was first registered, not the month it was ordered, paid for or handed
      over. A vehicle can be registered before it is sold.{" "}
      <a href="/method#data-grains">What the charts count</a>.{" "}
      <a href="/caveats">Registered is not the same as sold</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Survivors are not registrations</h2>
    <p>
      Most charts count vehicles first registered in a month that are still registered at the latest snapshot.
      Vehicles that have left the register since are not in the count, so a count for an older month is smaller than the
      number first registered then. The charts of true registrations count every vehicle first registered, including
      those since removed, and the two are never drawn together. <a href="/method#data-grains">The ways to count the register</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Scope can differ</h2>
    <p>
      This site counts only some vehicle types, and only vehicles registered in New Zealand for the first time.
      A source that counts other types, or counts returning vehicles, will give a different total. The default
      scope of true registrations leaves out buses, motor caravans and special purpose vehicles, among others, so
      its total of new vehicles is lower than a total that counts them. True registrations are also counted on a
      wider scope that takes those three in, as the next section says, and the charts of vehicles still registered
      count them too.{" "}
      <a href="/method#vehicle-scope">Which vehicles are counted</a>.
    </p>

    <ComparisonSection />

    <h2>The Motor Industry Association's figures</h2>
    <p>
      The Motor Industry Association (MIA) publishes a count of new vehicle registrations each month. Set beside
      it, this site's count of new vehicles on its default scope is lower: the published totals sit above it by 1.4%
      to 3.9% in the periods compared, which are eight months and three whole years between March 2021 and October
      2026, measured in November 2026. The total for October 2026 was read from the MIA's own page; the earlier
      ones are as quoted in trade press reports of its releases. The default scope is passenger cars and vans and
      goods vans, utes and trucks.
    </p>
    <p>
      Counted on the wider scope, which also takes in buses, motor caravans and special purpose vehicles, this
      site's count of new vehicles comes within 0.06% of the published total for October 2026, and within 1.3% for
      each of the other periods compared. The older the month, the wider the gap. The cause is not established.
      What has been measured is how close the counts come. It does not say how any organisation defines its own
      count, which has not been read here. The MIA's figures are as reported when a month closed, or as it later
      restates them; this site's are as NZTA's dashboard holds them at the latest export, where NZTA says a past
      month can gain or lose vehicles. The charts that count vehicles still registered are lower again, as the
      section above says. This site does not adjust a figure to match another source.
    </p>
    <p>
      The wider total sits beside the default one. In Explore it is under True registrations. On the canvas,
      choose True registrations, wider scope under Counting, then add New or used under Filters and choose NZ New.{" "}
      <a href={registrations}>True registrations in Explore</a>.{" "}
      <a href="/canvas/">Open the canvas</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The Motor Trade Association's figures</h2>
    <p>
      The Motor Trade Association's vstat site (vstat.mta.org.nz) says it is built from NZTA data. Compared on 17 November
      2026, it shows for October 2026 the same count of new passenger cars and vans as this site's true
      registrations, 11,824, and the same top ten new models.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its hybrid figure is higher than this site's hybrid line, and equal to this site's hybrid and mild hybrid
      lines together: for new passenger cars and vans in October 2026, 4,978 there, against 4,472 hybrid and 506
      mild hybrid here. This site shows mild hybrids as a line of their own. How
      vstat defines a hybrid has not been read here; what has been measured is that the figures agree when the two
      lines are added.
    </p>
    <p>
      The canvas can show the two as one group, as a count or as a share: put “powertrain group” in Break down by.{" "}
      <a href="/canvas/?canvas.charts=c1&c1.measure=share&c1.by=powertrain_group&c1.size=full">Open a share by powertrain group on the canvas</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Other sites take a different source</h2>
    <p>
      Some other sites take first registrations from NZTA's registrations dashboard. This site reads that dashboard
      too, for the charts of true registrations where a build has them, and the open register for everything else. A site that counts only
      the dashboard, or only the register, will give a different figure for the same month.{" "}
      <a href="/method#true-registrations">What true registrations count</a>.{" "}
      <a href="/caveats">Other limits of the data</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>If a figure here still looks wrong</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/feedback">Report it</a>, with the page address and the figure you expected.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
